Raise the user-element matrix to the encoding limit (150 -> 765 DOF), and
zero it over its real extent.

Two defects, both found by the sphere campaign, both invisible on the
layered decks that had been the only test.

1. THE CAPACITY WAS STILL TOO SMALL, AND THE NUMBER WAS A GUESS.

Patch 0006 raised the user-element matrix from 60 to 150 DOF (50 nodes)
on the evidence then available: rings of 4..32 on the sphere cell and
37, later 40, on the layered campaign cell.  50 nodes looked like ample
headroom.

It is not.  A U6 patch spans the centre node's whole 1-ring, so its size
is set by mesh valence and nothing bounds it.  The BSW_L050 sphere cell
contains a node carried by 248 tets, giving a 127-node ring -- 381 DOF.
The guard from 0006 fired correctly and stopped the run (at 51 nodes,
the first patch over the limit), which is the only reason this was a
clean failure and not another silently corrupted matrix.

The distribution is the point: of 37577 patches, 96% are 11 nodes or
fewer, ~30 exceed 50, and one reaches 127.  No percentile of a valence
histogram is a safe bound, so stop picking one.  ccx stores a user
element's node count in the single label byte lakon(8:8), and
userelements.f already rejects NODES > 255.  So 255 nodes -- 765 DOF --
is the largest patch ccx can express at all, and the matrix is now
dimensioned for exactly that:

  mafillsm.f     nduser 150 -> 765.  su/smu stay ALLOCATABLE, which
                 matters more at this size than before: 765*765*8 =
                 4.7 MB each, and a fixed local that large would be
                 placed in .bss and shared across the assembly threads
                 (see 0007).  Verified: .bss is still 0 in mafillsm.o,
                 mafilldmss.o and e_c3d_u6.o.
  mafilldmss.f   ffu(765), su/smu allocated (765,765).
  e_c3d_u*.f     s(765,765)/sm(765,765)/ff(765) throughout the family --
                 u, u1, u4, u5, u6, us3, us45 -- so every callee of
                 e_c3d_u agrees on the stride.
  e_c3d_u6.f     the guard is now `nope > 255` and says so: a ring that
                 trips it cannot be a ccx user element, and the mesh is
                 what has to change.
  nodalbbar.py   the same 255 limit, checked at generation, where the
                 offending node can be named.

Cost is 9.4 MB per assembly thread, paid once per mafillsm call.

2. THE MATRIX WAS ZEROED OVER A FIXED 60, NOT ITS REAL EXTENT.

e_c3d_u6 opened with

      do i=1,60
        ff(i)=0.d0
        do j=1,60
          s(i,j)=0.d0
        enddo
      enddo

which was correct when the array was (60,60) and is not correct now.
The stiffness loop afterwards assigns (not accumulates) the whole
3*nope square, so s survives; ff does NOT.  e_c3d_u6 never writes ff at
all, so for any patch wider than 20 nodes, ff(61..3*nope) was whatever
the previous element left there -- and mafillsm reads ffval=ffu(jj)
UNCONDITIONALLY, before it tests rhsi.  The caller allocates s/sm/ff
once and never re-zeroes between elements, so this is live data, not
zeros.

Layered rings reach 40 and sphere rings 127, so every U6 result taken
before this fix is suspect and was re-run.

The same two early returns (stiffness.eq.0, va.le.0) leave s itself
partly stale past row 60.

Fixed by zeroing 1..3*nope, and by zeroing sm as well: e_c3d_u6 is
static-only and never writes it, so it took sm as an argument (the
dispatcher in e_c3d_u.f now passes it) rather than leaving mafillsm to
read an untouched array whenever mass or buckling is on.

VERIFICATION

  * The deck that crashed -- BSW_L050 undrained, 320165 elements,
    153839 equations, a 127-node ring -- now solves in 12 s with
    equilibrium_gap 2.2e-07.
  * Small U6 cases are bit-identical run to run at fixed thread count,
    so there is no race.  Across thread counts they move by 1-3 units
    in the last printed digit (~1e-7 relative), which is cond*eps for a
    K/G = 5000 brine phase under a threaded direct solver's pivot
    reordering, not a correctness failure.
